  "prompt": "Bug: triggering a recipe action that posts to a local or blocked address answers 202, and nothing is sent.\n\nA \"post\" recipe action whose URL points at a loopback or private address (http://127.0.0.1:8742/, http://192.168.1.10/hook, http://[::1]/) can be triggered with POST /api/households/recipe-actions/{item_id}/trigger/{recipe_slug}. The endpoint answers 202 and queues the POST as a background task. safehttp then refuses the target inside that task, so the request is dropped and the user never learns why.\n\nExpected:\n- safehttp gets validate_url(url, *, allow_hosts=(), deny_hosts=()), exported from the package like post. It checks a URL against the same rules the safe transport applies when sending, without sending anything. The host is resolved (an IP literal is used as is, with no DNS lookup). Non-public addresses are blocked unless allowed. allow_hosts and deny_hosts take the same entries as post's (host names, addresses, CIDR networks), and the deny list wins. It returns None when the request would be allowed. It raises safehttp's InvalidDomainError when the request would be blocked or the host does not resolve. Examples: a host resolving to 10.0.0.5 is blocked; http://127.0.0.1:8742/ is blocked; a host resolving to 93.184.216.34 is allowed; a host resolving to 10.0.0.5 is allowed when its name is in allow_hosts, but blocked again when deny_hosts also holds \"10.0.0.0/8\". The transport's own checks at send time stay as they are.\n- The trigger endpoint checks a post action's URL before queueing it, with the same allow and deny lists it passes to post (HTTP_ALLOW_LIST and HTTP_DISALLOW_LIST, read through get_app_settings()). A blocked target answers 400 with the usual error body, whose detail.message mentions HTTP_ALLOW_LIST so the admin knows how to allow a trusted local host. Nothing is posted. With HTTP_ALLOW_LIST set to 127.0.0.1, an action on http://127.0.0.1:8742/ still answers 202 and posts once to that URL with allow_hosts [\"127.0.0.1\"].\n- Tests replace safehttp.post, safehttp.validate_url and the get_app_settings that the recipe-action routes use, so the endpoint must call each through that name at request time. The other trigger tests replace validate_url with a no-op, so their example.com actions still answer 202.\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests/pkgs/safehttp tests/integration_tests/user_household_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).",
  "title": "fix: report blocked recipe action targets instead of dropping them silently (#8747)",
